## BPS states, central charges, solitons and monopoles

The sibling supersymmetry note owns the super-Poincaré algebra and the
model-specific operator positivity derivation of `M≥|Z|`. This note consumes
that precise interface only after it states the spacetime dimension/signature,
supercharge anticommutators, normalization and allowed central/topological
charges. A BPS state must be defined as a state in a specified Hilbert-space
representation saturating the bound, equivalently annihilated by the relevant
positive supercharge combination. “BPS multiplet,” “shortening,” “protected
sector,” “central charge,” “charge lattice” and “index” each need exact model
definitions. A degeneracy is not generally an index, and an index can exhibit
cancellation or continuum contributions.

Define the Witten index only when the trace exists, for example
`Tr_H((-1)^F e^{-βH})` on a specified `Z_2`-graded Hilbert space with
trace-class heat kernel; then prove its `β`-independence using the exact
supercharge relation and trace assumptions. A superconformal index additionally
specifies `H(S^3)`, the chosen supercharge, the positive anticommutator whose
kernel is counted, all commuting fugacity charges, and convergence/regularity.
It is not the dimension of the full Hilbert space. In 4D `N=2` a second
helicity supertrace or refined protected spin character needs its exact angular
momentum/refinement convention and a proof that the chosen sector is protected.

For a classical monopole example, fix a `3+1`-dimensional Minkowski model with
compact gauge group, adjoint Higgs field `φ`, gauge connection `A`, coupling,
zero Higgs potential (Prasad–Sommerfield limit), finite-energy static fields
and a chosen vacuum `φ_∞` that breaks `G` to a declared unbroken subgroup.
Define `B_i=\frac12 ε_{ijk}F_{jk}`, `D_iφ`, the asymptotic map/transition data
and magnetic charge `m=∫_{S^2_∞}⟨φ,B_i⟩dS_i`. For the action convention
`E=(1/(2g^2))∫_{R^3}(|B|^2+|Dφ|^2)d^3x`, put `T=g^{-2}m` and require
smooth fields and falloff such
that `D_iB_i=0`, `D_iφ→0` at infinity, the asymptotic magnetic field lies in
the unbroken algebra, the surface flux exists, and integration by parts has no
residual bulk/boundary term. With an invariant inner product the displayed
surface charge is gauge invariant under the allowed asymptotic gauge group.
Then both square completions hold:

