items/thm-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-block-decomposition.md:112:6.1 [Exhaustion and the decomposition] Iterate step 5.1: as long as the orthogonal complement of the blocks already produced is nonzero, step 2.1 supplies a largest eigenvalue and step 4.1 a further nonzero invariant block of dimension $2j_\nu+1\ge1$, orthogonally split off, decreasing the dimension of the complement by $2j_\nu+1$. Since the dimension is a nonnegative integer, the process terminates after some finite number $r\ge1$ of blocks with complement $\{0\}$, yielding an orthogonal decomposition $V=\bigoplus_{\nu=1}^rV_{\nu}$ and an orthonormal basis $(v_{\nu,k})$ as in (b); the basis is orthonormal as a union of the block bases because the blocks are pairwise orthogonal, and $\sum_\nu(2j_\nu+1)=\dim V$ by additivity of dimensions in orthogonal direct sums [F5], [F7]. Each block action is the spin-$j_\nu$ action by step 4.1 and [F6], which proves (b); step 3.1 applied to a block identifies the largest eigenvalue of $J_z$ on that block as $j_\nu$, completing (c) except for $\lambda\ge0$, proved in step 2.1. [step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 5.1, F5, F6, F7, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-block-decomposition.md:114:7.1 [Multiplicity count] Let $M\in\frac12\mathbb N$ and consider the eigenvalue-$M$ eigenspace of $J_z$. A block with parameter $j$ contributes the basis vector with $k=j-M$ exactly when $j\in M+\mathbb Z$ and $0\le M\le j$, and contributes nothing in all other cases (its weights are $j,j-1,\dots,-j$, all in the coset $j+\mathbb Z$); hence $\dim\ker(J_z-MI)=\#\{\nu:j_\nu\in M+\mathbb Z,\ j_\nu\ge M\}$ by the orthonormal basis of step 6.1. Applying this at $M$ and at $M+1$, both in the same coset $M+\mathbb Z$, and subtracting gives $\#\{\nu:j_\nu=M\}=\dim\ker(J_z-MI)-\dim\ker(J_z-(M+1)I)$. The multiset $\{j_1,\dots,j_r\}$ is therefore determined by $J_z$ (using the fixed relations to know that the decomposition exists), and the spectrum statement follows by collecting the eigenvalues $j_\nu-k$ of the blocks; the coset qualifier in (d) excludes nothing, since blocks outside $M+\mathbb Z$ contribute no vector to either eigenspace. This proves (d). [step 6.1, algebra] ∎
items/ex-qa-24-rank-one-pue.md:39:**Given:** $\sigma\in[0,1]$, the standard orthonormal basis $|0\rangle,|1\rangle$ of $\mathbb C^2$, the PUE definition, and the ordered QSVT sequence.
items/cex-smooth-interior-data-do-not-repair-incompatible-dirichlet-corner-values.md:61:Hunter's Theorems 4.30-4.31 (printed pp. 114-116) are stated for zero-trace classes, so the inhomogeneous datum must first be lifted; Simon's Lecture 9 Theorem 1 (printed pp. 88-90) likewise assumes a localized zero-Dirichlet class; it is not a source for the square's incompatible sidewise data. The two-limit contradiction at the corner is elementary and uses only continuity; the $H^2$ clause additionally uses the Sobolev embedding of [F2], which is why this item states the Axiom of Choice even though the primary refutation of continuous solutions needs none.
items/ex-qsmx-ising-classical-and-field-limits.md:63:[F3] [[lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus]]: every finite Hermitian matrix has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal eigenbasis, and the finite trace is basis-independent.
