items/ex-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-two-level-precession.md:94:Let $H=\mathbb C^{2}$ carry the standard inner product $\langle z,w\rangle=z_{1}\overline{w_{1}}+z_{2}\overline{w_{2}}$ ([[def-inner-product-space]], [[def-hilbert-space]], [[cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm]]), with orthonormal basis $u_{1}=(1,0)$, $u_{2}=(0,1)$ and rank-one calculus $T_{u,v}x=\langle x,v\rangle u$ ([[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]]). Fix two distinct energies $E_{1}\ne E_{2}$ in joules (model parameters, not measured values), and consider the assigned energy observable of [[def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution]] clause (a),
items/ex-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-two-level-precession.md:119:**Given:** The Hilbert space $H=\mathbb C^{2}$ with the standard inner product and orthonormal basis $(u_{1},u_{2})$; distinct real energies $E_{1}\ne E_{2}$ in joules, and the reduced Planck constant $\hbar$ with its value and unit, in the framework's quantity conventions ([[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]); the assigned energy observable $H_{\mathrm{op}}=E_{1}T_{u_{1},u_{1}}+E_{2}T_{u_{2},u_{2}}$ with unit $\mathrm J$; the observables $X,Y,Z$; a unit vector $\psi_{0}=\alpha u_{1}+\beta u_{2}$; the number $\omega=(E_{2}-E_{1})/\hbar$; the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]), the choice hypotheses of the cited spectral suppliers.
items/ex-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-two-level-precession.md:121:[F1] **Rank-one calculus and one-dimensional projections.** $T_{u,v}x=\langle x,v\rangle u$ is bounded with $T_{u,v}^{*}=T_{v,u}$, and the assignment is linear in $u$ and conjugate-linear in $v$; for every nonzero $\psi$ the operator $T_{\psi,\psi}$ is self-adjoint positive trace class, and for every unit $u$ the operator $T_{u,u}$ is the Hilbert orthogonal projection onto the one-dimensional subspace $\mathbb Cu$, so that $T_{u,u}$ is idempotent and $T_{u,u}\psi=\langle\psi,u\rangle u$ ([[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]], [[lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection]] clause (b)).
items/ex-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-two-level-precession.md:125:[F7] **Resolution of the identity.** Since $(u_{1},u_{2})$ is an orthonormal basis of the two-dimensional space $H$, every $v\in H$ satisfies $v=\langle v,u_{1}\rangle u_{1}+\langle v,u_{2}\rangle u_{2}$, and therefore $T_{u_{1},u_{1}}+T_{u_{2},u_{2}}=I$ ([[thm-bessel-inequality-and-finite-parseval-identity]]).
items/pthm-nrqmx-jaynes-cummings-blocks.md:95:[F3] The completed ordered Hilbert tensor product has the product orthonormal basis; atom and field operators act on separate factors and commute on the algebraic core ([[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]]).
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-explicit-unbounded-oscillator-propagator.md:77:**Given:** CC, $m,\omega,\hbar>0$, the exact oscillator $H_{\mathrm{osc}}$, its Hermite eigenbasis $(\psi_n)$ and graph domain $Y$, the real $C^1$ force $f$, and the Weyl formula in the Statement. Inner products are linear in the first variable. Schwartz space $\mathcal S(\mathbb R)$ consists of smooth $u$ with $\sup_x|x^j\partial_x^ku(x)|<\infty$ for every pair of nonnegative integers $j,k$. A strong solution means an $L^2$-norm $C^1$ curve taking values in $Y$ and satisfying the equation there; uniqueness is asserted in this class for each initial vector in $Y$. Mild evolution on $L^2$ means the continuous unitary extension of these strong solutions.
items/lem-qa-16-filtered-gamma2-well-defined.md:49:[F4] [[cor-finite-dimensional-inner-product-spaces-have-orthonormal-bases]]: every finite-dimensional real or complex inner-product space has an orthonormal basis.
items/lem-qa-16-filtered-gamma2-well-defined.md:61:2.1 For any feasible factorization in finite dimension, take, for each i, the span of the d vectors $u_{x,i}$ and d vectors $v_{y,i}$. It has dimension at most $2d$; by [F4] choose an orthonormal basis and map its vectors to their coordinates, padding with zero coordinates in $\mathbb C^{2d}$. This preserves every inner product and norm. Thus every feasible candidate has a representation in one fixed finite product of coordinate spaces $\mathbb C^{2d}$. [given, F1, F4, step 1.1, construct]
items/lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition.md:74:[L3] In an $\eta$-orthonormal ordered basis of a Lorentz vector space, the coordinate expression is $\eta(v,v)=-(v^0)^2+\lvert v_{\mathrm{vec}}\rvert^2$ with $v=v^0e_0+v^1e_1+v^2e_2+v^3e_3$, the timelike set is $T=T_+\cup T_-$ with $T_+=\{v:v^0>\lvert v_{\mathrm{vec}}\rvert\}$ and $T_-=\{v:v^0<-\lvert v_{\mathrm{vec}}\rvert\}$ the two connected components, and these descriptions coincide with the two components in some order in every orthonormal basis ([[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]]).
items/lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition.md:90:1.2 By [L2] choose an ordered $g_p$-orthonormal basis $(f_0,f_1,f_2,f_3)$. The vector $f_0$ is timelike, and [L4] gives $g_p(f_0,N_p)\neq0$; replacing $f_0$ by $-f_0$ if necessary, which preserves orthonormality, we may assume $g_p(f_0,N_p)<0$, so that $f_0\in C_p^+$ and $f_0$ is future-directed. By [L3] the two components of the timelike set are $T_+=\{v:v^0>\lvert v_{\mathrm{vec}}\rvert\}$ and $T_-=\{v:v^0<-\lvert v_{\mathrm{vec}}\rvert\}$ in this basis, in some order; since $f_0$ has coordinates $(1,0,0,0)\in T_+$ and lies in $C_p^+$, the identification is $C_p^+=T_+$. Writing $n=n^0f_0+n^1f_1+n^2f_2+n^3f_3$ by [L6] and using that $n$ is future unit timelike, we get $n^0>\lvert n_{\mathrm{vec}}\rvert\ge0$ and $(n^0)^2-\lvert n_{\mathrm{vec}}\rvert^2=1$. [L2, L3, L4, L5, L6, given]
items/thm-schur-orthogonality-for-compact-groups.md:93:[F5] Orthonormal expansions: for an orthonormal basis $e_1,\dots,e_d$ of a
items/thm-schur-orthogonality-for-compact-groups.md:128:1.4 The rank-one trace is $\operatorname{tr}(T)=\langle v,v'\rangle$: in the orthonormal basis $e_1,\dots,e_d$, the matrix of $T$ has entries $\langle Te_j,e_i\rangle=\langle e_j,v'\rangle\langle v,e_i\rangle$, so $\operatorname{tr}(T)=\sum_{i=1}^d\langle e_i,v'\rangle\langle v,e_i\rangle=\sum_{i=1}^d\langle v,e_i\rangle\langle e_i,v'\rangle=\bigl\langle\sum_{i=1}^d\langle v,e_i\rangle e_i,v'\bigr\rangle=\langle v,v'\rangle$, pulling the finite sum out of the first variable by [F5] and using the orthonormal expansion $v=\sum_{i=1}^d\langle v,e_i\rangle e_i$ of [F5]. [F4, F5]
items/thm-schur-orthogonality-for-compact-groups.md:132:2.2 The trace of $A(T)$ computes $c$ and the trace of $T$: by [F4] applied to an orthonormal basis $e_1,\dots,e_d$ of $V$, $\operatorname{tr}(A(T))=\sum_{i=1}^d\langle A(T)e_i,e_i\rangle=\sum_{i=1}^d\int_K\langle\pi(k)T\pi(k)^{-1}e_i,e_i\rangle\,d\mu(k)=\int_K\operatorname{tr}\bigl(\pi(k)T\pi(k)^{-1}\bigr)d\mu(k)=\int_K\operatorname{tr}(T)\,d\mu(k)=\operatorname{tr}(T)$, using the weak pairing formula of [F3] for $\langle A(T)e_i,e_i\rangle$, termwise integration by [F6], the trace of the conjugated endomorphism in [F4], and $\mu(K)=1$ in [F6]. On the other hand $\operatorname{tr}(A(T))=\operatorname{tr}(cI)=cd$ by [F4]. [F3, F4, F6, step 1.3]
items/cor-bounded-map-into-h-one-zero-followed-by-rellich-is-compact-on-ltwo.md:65:2.1 Since every bounded sequence in $L^2(\Omega)$ has an image under $\iota T$ with a convergent subsequence, [F3] makes $\iota T$ a compact operator. The inclusion $\iota$ is bounded because $\|u\|_{L^2}\le\|u\|_{H^1_0}$, and the Axiom of Choice is inherited through the Rellich theorem [F1]. [F1, F3, step 1.1] ∎ 
