+
+## Definition
+
+Use the complex Hilbert-space convention that the inner product is linear in its first variable and conjugate-linear in its second. Let $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and let $\sigma^\mu$ be the matrices in the local four-dimensional algebra convention. Define $\Phi:\mathbb R^{1,3}\to\operatorname{Herm}_2(\mathbb C)$ by $\Phi(x)=-\sigma^\mu x_\mu$. For $g\in\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb C)=\{g\in M_2(\mathbb C):\det g=1\}$, define $\Lambda_g$ by $\Phi(\Lambda_gx)=g\Phi(x)g^\dagger$; the real-linear map $\Phi$ is an isomorphism because its four matrix coefficients form a basis of the Hermitian $2\times2$ matrices, and $\det\Phi(x)=-\eta_{\mu\nu}x^\mu x^\nu$ is preserved. Let $\Lambda_g^\vee$ be the dual action on covectors, $(\Lambda_g^\vee p)(x)=p(\Lambda_g^{-1}x)$. The spin-cover Poincare group is $\mathcal P=\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb C)\ltimes_\Lambda\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with product $(g,a)(h,b)=(gh,a+\Lambda_gb)$.
+
+A **positive unitary super-Poincare representation** in this item is the following data. It includes a nonzero complex Hilbert space $\mathcal H$, a bounded self-adjoint grading $\Gamma$ with $\Gamma^2=I$, and a strongly continuous unitary representation $U$ of $\mathcal P$ commuting with $\Gamma$. It also includes a regular projection-valued measure $E$ on the Borel subsets of the covector space $(\mathbb R^{1,3})^*$ such that
+
+$$U(1,a)=\Phi_E(p\mapsto e^{i p(a)}),\qquad U(g,0)E(B)U(g,0)^*=E(\Lambda_g^\vee B),\qquad E(\mathcal C_+^*)=I,$$
+
+where $\Phi_E$ is the bounded Borel PVM integral, $\mathcal C_+^*=\{p:p^0=-p_0\ge |\vec p|\}$ is the closed future cone in covector coordinates, and $p(a)=p_\mu a^\mu$. For each coordinate define the self-adjoint translation generator
+
+$$P_\mu=p_\mu(E),\qquad D(P_\mu)=\left\{\psi\in\mathcal H:\int |p_\mu|^2\,dE_\psi(p)<\infty\right\},\qquad E_\psi(B)=\langle E(B)\psi,\psi\rangle.$$
+
+Here $p_\mu(E)$ is the unbounded PVM integral with its truncation domain; the real-valued integral is self-adjoint under Countable Choice. Thus $P^0=\eta^{0\nu}P_\nu$ is nonnegative. The joint PVM is part of the representation data; no joint spectral measure is inferred from formal commutators on a dense domain.
+
+For each $I,\alpha$, the supercharge $Q^I_\alpha$ is a closed densely defined operator on $\mathcal H$ and $\bar Q_{\dot\alpha I}=(Q^I_\alpha)^*$ is its actual Hilbert adjoint. The central generators $Z^{IJ}$ and $\bar Z_{IJ}$ are represented by closed densely defined operators related by the same adjoint pairing. There is a common dense graded linear domain $\mathcal D$ contained in the domains of every generator product in the bracket table; it is invariant under those generators and under $U(\mathcal P)$, and the displayed super-Poincare relations hold there. The even group acts on the charge domains exactly: for every $g\in\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb C)$, conjugation by $U(g,0)$ sends each closed charge to the corresponding fundamental or dotted spinor transform as an equality of operators, including equality of domains. The central generators transform as Lorentz scalars.
+
+For every Borel set $B\subseteq(\mathbb R^{1,3})^*$ and every charge $Q$ or $Q^*$, require the reducing-projection/domain conditions
+
+$$E(B)D(Q)\subseteq D(Q),\qquad Q E(B)\psi=E(B)Q\psi\quad(\psi\in D(Q)),$$
+
+and the same conditions with $Q^*$ in place of $Q$. In particular this supplies integrated translation covariance with domain invariance; a relation $[P_\mu,Q]=0$ on a common core alone is not substituted for it. The representation is irreducible when its only closed graded subspaces invariant under the integrated even group and the charge domains/actions are $\{0\}$ and $\mathcal H$. Countable Choice is the only choice principle used by the PVM and unbounded-integral definitions here; no conclusion of Stone's theorem or the unbounded self-adjoint spectral theorem is invoked to construct $E$.
+
+## Definition references
+
+[Martin, §§1–4.4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9709356v7).
+
