+---
+id: thm-qftx-supermultiplet-mass-degeneracy
+kind: theorem
+title: Mass is constant on an irreducible positive super-Poincare representation
+status: draft
+origin: pipeline
+pipeline_run: advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+category: quantum-field-theory-mathematics
+deps:
+  - def-qftx-unitary-super-poincare-representation
+  - def-projection-valued-measure
+  - lem-scalar-and-complex-measures-from-a-pvm
+  - def-countable-choice
+  - thm-of-square-roots
+dependency_roles:
+  def-qftx-unitary-super-poincare-representation: mathematical-premise
+  def-projection-valued-measure: mathematical-premise
+  lem-scalar-and-complex-measures-from-a-pvm: mathematical-premise
+  def-countable-choice: mathematical-premise
+  thm-of-square-roots: mathematical-premise
+dependency_level: 2
+proof_strategy: direct
+provenance:
+  statement: ai-altered
+  proof: ai-generated
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: "Stephen P. Martin, A Supersymmetry Primer, version 7"
+      url: "https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9709356v7"
+      locator: "§1, printed pp. 5–6, mass equality within a supermultiplet; the local proof adds exact PVM and charge-domain hypotheses."
+    - title: "S. J. Gates Jr., M. T. Grisaru, M. Roček and W. Siegel, Superspace, or One Thousand and One Lessons in Supersymmetry"
+      url: "https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0108200"
+      locator: "§3.3, printed pp. 69–73, Poincare mass as a Casimir in particle representations; the local proof avoids a sharp-momentum vector."
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Let $\mathcal H$ carry an irreducible positive unitary super-Poincare representation with metric $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, future-cone support and the charge-domain reduction conditions stated in its definition. Define $s:(\mathbb R^{1,3})^*\to[0,\infty)$ by $s(p)=-\eta^{\mu\nu}p_\mu p_\nu$ on the closed future cone and define its pushforward projection-valued measure by $F(A)=E(s^{-1}(A))$ for Borel $A\subseteq[0,\infty)$. Then every supercharge and its adjoint reduce each mass-squared spectral subspace: if $\psi\in D(Q)\cap\operatorname{ran}F(A)$ then $Q\psi\in\operatorname{ran}F(A)$, and the same holds for $Q^*$. Moreover there is a unique $m^2\ge0$ such that $F(A)=I$ when $m^2\in A$ and $F(A)=0$ when $m^2\notin A$. Thus the nonnegative mass $m=\sqrt{m^2}$ is constant throughout the irreducible multiplet. For a nonzero supercharge image, the source and image lie in the same mass spectral sector. No sharp-momentum eigenvector or rest frame is assumed in the full Hilbert space. If an additional group acts unitarily and commutes with the supercharges on their invariant domains, each supercharge also intertwines that group action; no gauge-charge conclusion is asserted without this extra hypothesis.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Martin, §1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9709356v7).
+
+[Gates et al., §3.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0108200).
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** An irreducible positive unitary super-Poincare representation as in the Statement, and its joint momentum PVM $E$.
+
+[F1] [[def-qftx-unitary-super-poincare-representation]]: $E$ is a joint PVM with future-cone support, it is covariant under the even Lorentz group, it commutes with translations, and every charge and adjoint has the stated reducing-projection/domain property for each $E(B)$.
+
+[F2] [[def-projection-valued-measure]]: each $E(B)$ is an orthogonal projection and $E$ is strongly countably additive on disjoint Borel sets.
+
+[F3] [[lem-scalar-and-complex-measures-from-a-pvm]]: for any unit vector $\psi$, $\mu_\psi(A)=\langle F(A)\psi,\psi\rangle$ is a countably additive probability measure once $F$ is a normalized PVM.
+
+[F4] [[thm-of-square-roots]]: every nonnegative real number has a unique nonnegative square root.
+
+## Proof
+
+**Proof technique:** direct.
+
+**Given:** $\mathcal H$, $U$, $\Gamma$, the charge operators, and $E$ as in [F1].
+
+1.1 The function $s(p)=-\eta^{\mu\nu}p_\mu p_\nu=(p^0)^2-|\vec p|^2$ is continuous and nonnegative on the future cone in [F1]. For each Borel $A\subseteq[0,\infty)$, $s^{-1}(A)$ is Borel; therefore $F(A):=E(s^{-1}(A))$ is a normalized PVM on $[0,\infty)$, since inverse images preserve disjoint unions and $E$ is strongly countably additive. [F1, F2, algebra]
+
+1.2 For each Borel $A$, $F(A)$ equals one of the projections $E(s^{-1}(A))$. The charge-domain reduction in [F1] therefore gives $F(A)D(Q)\subseteq D(Q)$ and $QF(A)\psi=F(A)Q\psi$ for every $\psi\in D(Q)$; the same argument applies to $Q^*$. This proves the spectral-subspace assertion. [F1, step 1.1]
+
+2.1 Since $s(\Lambda_g^\vee p)=s(p)$, Lorentz covariance of $E$ implies $U(g,0)F(A)U(g,0)^*=F(A)$. Translation unitaries are bounded Borel functions of $E$, so they commute with every $F(A)$; $\Gamma$ commutes with $E$ by [F1]. Hence $\operatorname{ran}F(A)$ is closed and graded, invariant under the integrated even group, and invariant under every charge and adjoint with dense restricted domains: density follows by applying the bounded projection $F(A)$ to a dense operator domain. Irreducibility as defined in [F1] forces $F(A)=0$ or $F(A)=I$. [F1, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.2 Choose any unit vector $\psi\in\mathcal H$ and set $\mu(A)=\langle F(A)\psi,\psi\rangle$. By [F3] this is a countably additive probability measure, and by step 2.1 every value is $0$ or $1$. The set $R=\{r\ge0:\mu([0,r])=1\}$ is nonempty: otherwise the countable union $[0,\infty)=\bigcup_{n\ge1}[0,n]$ would have measure zero. Put $m^2=\inf R$, which is a finite nonnegative real. [F1, F3, step 2.1]
+
+2.3 For every $\varepsilon>0$, the infimum property gives $r\in R$ with $r<m^2+\varepsilon$, so $\mu([0,m^2+\varepsilon])=1$. If $m^2>\varepsilon$, then $m^2-\varepsilon<m^2$ is not in $R$, and the zero-or-one property gives $\mu([0,m^2-\varepsilon])=0$. Thus for all sufficiently large $n$, the nested intervals $I_n=(\max(0,m^2-1/n),m^2+1/n]$ have measure one; when $m^2=0$, use $I_n=[0,1/n]$. Countable additivity of the finite measure gives continuity from above, hence $\mu(\{m^2\})=\lim_n\mu(I_n)=1$. Since $F(\{m^2\})$ is either zero or the identity, it must be $I$. Monotonicity and normalization then give $F(A)=I$ exactly when $m^2\in A$, and $F(A)=0$ otherwise. [F1, F3, step 2.1, step 2.2]
+
+3.1 By [F4], $m^2$ has a unique nonnegative square root $m$. Step 1.2 shows that each charge maps vectors in a fixed mass-squared subspace into that same subspace; step 2.3 shows there is only one such spectral value in the irreducible representation. The argument uses the pushforward PVM rather than a normalizable sharp-momentum vector, and it includes $m=0$ without introducing a rest frame. [F4, step 1.2, step 2.3]
+
+3.2 If an additional unitary group $V(g)$ preserves $D(Q)$ and satisfies $V(g)Q\psi=QV(g)\psi$ there, then for every $\psi\in D(Q)$ one has $V(g)(Q\psi)=Q(V(g)\psi)$; this is exactly the intertwining relation. Without that additional commutation hypothesis no gauge representation equality follows. [given, algebra] ∎
+
+## Remarks
+
+The theorem fixes the invariant-mass support of an irreducible representation and the mass sector of every nonzero supercharge image. It asserts no multiplicity formula and no physical particle existence or stability result.
