1.1 At rest $P_0=-M I$ and $P_j=0$, so [F1] gives $\{Q^I_1,(Q^J_1)^*\}=2M\delta_{IJ}I$ and $\{Q^I_2,(Q^J_2)^*\}=2M\delta_{IJ}I$. Put $q_I=Q^I_1$ and $r_I=(Q^I_2)^*$; then $\{q_I,r_J^*\}=2z^{IJ}I$ and $\{r_J,q_I^*\}=2\bar z_{IJ}I$, with all these products defined on $\mathcal D$. [A1, F1, F2, given]

1.2 For $u,v\in\mathbb C^N$, define on $\mathcal D$ the charge combination $B_{u,v}=\sum_I\bar u_Iq_I+\sum_I\bar v_Ir_I$ and the combination of its summand adjoints $B^\sharp_{u,v}=\sum_Iu_Iq_I^*+\sum_Iv_Ir_I^*$. Expanding the algebraic anticommutator on $\mathcal D$ gives $B_{u,v}B^\sharp_{u,v}+B^\sharp_{u,v}B_{u,v}=\left(2M(\|u\|_2^2+\|v\|_2^2)+4\operatorname{Re}(u^*Zv)\right)I$. The diagonal blocks use the two mixed rest anticommutators in step 1.1, and the off-diagonal terms are $2u^*Zv$ and its complex conjugate. For every $\psi\in\mathcal D$, expanding the quadratic form term by term and using the adjoint identity for each individual charge gives $\langle(B_{u,v}B^\sharp_{u,v}+B^\sharp_{u,v}B_{u,v})\psi,\psi\rangle=\|B_{u,v}\psi\|^2+\|B^\sharp_{u,v}\psi\|^2\ge0$. [F2, step 1.1, algebra]

1.3 Fix a unit vector $v\in\mathbb C^N$. If $Zv\ne0$, choose $u=-Zv/\|Zv\|_2$, so $\|u\|_2=1$ and $u^*Zv=-\|Zv\|_2$. Applying step 1.2 to any nonzero $\psi\in\mathcal D$ yields $0\le4(M-\|Zv\|_2)\|\psi\|^2$, hence $M\ge\|Zv\|_2$. If $Zv=0$, the same inequality follows from $M>0$. Taking the supremum over all unit $v$ proves $M\ge\|Z\|_{\mathrm{op}}$. [given, step 1.2, algebra, choose]

2.1 When $N=2$, $Z=\begin{pmatrix}0&z\\-z&0\end{pmatrix}$, so direct multiplication gives $Z^*Z=|z|^2I_2$ and therefore $\|Zv\|_2=|z|\|v\|_2$ for every $v$; thus $\|Z\|_{\mathrm{op}}=|z|$. With $q=Q^1_1$ and $r=Q^2_2$, expanding $A=q-e^{i\phi}r^*$ and $A^\sharp=q^*-e^{-i\phi}r$ gives $AA^\sharp+A^\sharp A=\{q,q^*\}+\{r^*,r\}-e^{-i\phi}\{q,r\}-e^{i\phi}\{r^*,q^*\}=2M+2M-2e^{-i\phi}z-2e^{i\phi}\bar z=4(M-|z|)I$ on $\mathcal D$. This proves the stated N=2 identity with the declared phase convention. [F1, F3, step 1.1, step 1.3, algebra]

2.2 If $M=|z|$, step 2.1 gives $AA^\sharp+A^\sharp A=0$ on $\mathcal D$. For every $\psi\in\mathcal D$, the actual-adjoint identity for the individual charge summands in [F2] gives $0=\langle(AA^\sharp+A^\sharp A)\psi,\psi\rangle=\|A\psi\|^2+\|A^\sharp\psi\|^2$. Each squared norm is nonnegative, so both vanish. If $\mathcal K$ is finite-dimensional, $A^\sharp=A^*$ and this is the ordinary operator-adjoint conclusion; in general it is the stated dense-domain conclusion. The result is confined to the fixed rest fiber and asserts no state existence or stability. [F2, step 2.1] ∎

## Remarks

For general $N$, the norm inequality does not by itself claim a multiplicity formula or a shortened representation. Those require additional representation-theoretic hypotheses and proofs.
1.1 The domain is a linear subspace: for $a,b\in\mathbb C$, the inequality $\lambda\|a\psi_\lambda+b\varphi_\lambda\|^2\le2|a|^2\lambda\|\psi_\lambda\|^2+2|b|^2\lambda\|\varphi_\lambda\|^2$ makes its defining sum finite. It contains every finite-support vector because each such sum is finite, so the stipulated density of finite-support vectors makes $D(Q)$ dense. Each $S_\lambda$ is self-adjoint and satisfies $S_\lambda^2=I$: the two diagonal blocks of its square are $U_\lambda^*U_\lambda=I$ and $U_\lambda U_\lambda^*=I$. Its off-diagonal form exchanges the two parity blocks, so it anticommutes with $\Gamma$. [given, F2, F5, algebra]

1.2 For $\psi,\varphi\in D(Q)$, the block series for $\langle Q\psi,\varphi\rangle$ converges absolutely by $\sum_\lambda\sqrt\lambda\|\psi_\lambda\|\|\varphi_\lambda\|\le\left(\sum_\lambda\lambda\|\psi_\lambda\|^2\right)^{1/2}\left(\sum_\lambda\|\varphi_\lambda\|^2\right)^{1/2}<\infty$. Self-adjointness of each finite-dimensional $S_\lambda$ and reality of $\sqrt\lambda$ then give $\langle Q\psi,\varphi\rangle=\langle\psi,Q\varphi\rangle$, including the zero block. Thus $Q$ is symmetric on its dense domain. [F2, step 1.1, algebra]

1.3 Let $\eta\in D(Q^*)$, with $\chi=Q^*\eta$. In [F3]'s identity, test first against vectors supported in one zero block and then against arbitrary vectors in a single $\lambda$ block. Nondegeneracy of the finite-dimensional inner product gives $\chi_0=0$ and $\chi_\lambda=\sqrt\lambda S_\lambda\eta_\lambda$ for every $\lambda$. Since $S_\lambda$ is unitary, $\|\chi\|^2=\sum_\lambda\lambda\|\eta_\lambda\|^2<\infty$, so $\eta\in D(Q)$. Conversely, for $\eta\in D(Q)$, step 1.2 gives $\langle Q\psi,\eta\rangle=\langle\psi,Q\eta\rangle$ for every $\psi\in D(Q)$, so [F3] gives $\eta\in D(Q^*)$ and $Q^*\eta=Q\eta$. Hence $D(Q)=D(Q^*)$ and $Q=Q^*$ by [F4]. [F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, step 1.2]

2.1 Squaring the block formula gives $Q^2=0$ on the zero block and $Q^2=\lambda I$ on $\mathcal H_\lambda^+\oplus\mathcal H_\lambda^-$. A vector belongs to $D(Q^2)$ exactly when it belongs to $D(Q)$ and $Q\psi$ belongs to $D(Q)$; using $\|S_\lambda\psi_\lambda\|=\|\psi_\lambda\|$, these conditions require both $\sum_\lambda\lambda\|\psi_\lambda\|^2<\infty$ and $\sum_\lambda\lambda^2\|\psi_\lambda\|^2<\infty$. For any $\psi\in\mathcal H$, the second sum implies the first because $\sum_{0<\lambda\le1}\lambda\|\psi_\lambda\|^2\le\sum_{0<\lambda\le1}\|\psi_\lambda\|^2$ and $\sum_{\lambda>1}\lambda\|\psi_\lambda\|^2\le\sum_{\lambda>1}\lambda^2\|\psi_\lambda\|^2$. Thus $D(Q^2)=\{\psi:\sum_\lambda\lambda^2\|\psi_\lambda\|^2<\infty\}$. The block anticommutation in step 1.1 gives $Q\Gamma=-\Gamma Q$ on $D(Q)$, which is invariant under $\Gamma$. [given, step 1.1, step 1.3, algebra]

2.2 On the domain displayed in step 2.1, $H=Q^2$ is symmetric because each diagonal scalar $\lambda$ is real. Testing the adjoint identity from [F3] on vectors supported in a single zero or positive block shows that a vector $\eta$ belongs to $D(H^*)$ exactly when the diagonal vector $(0,(\lambda\eta_\lambda))$ lies in $\mathcal H$, which is equivalent to $\sum_\lambda\lambda^2\|\eta_\lambda\|^2<\infty$. The adjoint then has the same diagonal values as $H$. Therefore $D(H^*)=D(H)$ and $H=H^*$ by [F4]. For every $\psi\in D(H)$, $\langle H\psi,\psi\rangle=\sum_\lambda\lambda\|\psi_\lambda\|^2\ge0$, so $H$ is nonnegative. [F2, F3, F4, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
1.1 The displayed occupation rules and their adjoints give the two-mode CAR on each of the four basis vectors: for equal mode labels, creation followed by annihilation and annihilation followed by creation are the projections onto occupied and empty states, whose sum is the identity; for distinct labels, the two orders of changing the different occupations have opposite signs from the factor $(-1)^{n_1}$. Repeated creation or annihilation is zero. Therefore $\{a_i,a_j^*\}=\delta_{ij}I$ and $\{a_i,a_j\}=\{a_i^*,a_j^*\}=0$. The same-mode mixed charges in the rest relation now give $2M I$, while every mismatched label is either a distinct-mode anticommutator or a repeated annihilator/creator and is zero. This verifies the mixed relation in [F1]. [given, F1, F4, algebra]

1.2 For the same-flavor pairs, two annihilators or two creators anticommute by step 1.1, so their central bracket is zero. The two nonzero distinct-flavor brackets are $\{Q^1_1,Q^2_2\}=2M e^{i\phi}\{a_1,a_1^*\}=2zI$ and $\{Q^1_2,Q^2_1\}=-2M e^{i\phi}\{a_2,a_2^*\}=-2zI$. Every other distinct-flavor pair is either a distinct-mode anticommutator or a square of one creation/annihilation operator, and so has zero anticommutator. These are exactly $2\epsilon_{\alpha\beta}Z^{IJ}$ with $\epsilon_{12}=1$ and antisymmetric $Z$; taking Hilbert adjoints gives the conjugate barred relations. [F1, step 1.1, algebra]

2.1 Since $(Q^2_2)^*=e^{-i\phi}\sqrt{2M}a_1$ and $(Q^2_1)^*=-e^{-i\phi}\sqrt{2M}a_2$, direct substitution gives $Q^1_1-e^{i\phi}(Q^2_2)^*=0$ and $Q^1_2+e^{i\phi}(Q^2_1)^*=0$. Also $|z|=M$, so the bound in [F2] is saturated and its stated annihilation conclusion agrees with these explicit zero operators. The Hilbert space has two even and two odd basis vectors, and all domains equal the full finite-dimensional space. [F2, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
1.1 The left derivatives with respect to distinct odd generators anticommute by [F2]; each $\partial_\mu$ commutes with them and with every other $\partial_\nu$ because the coefficient functions are smooth. Expanding the displayed formulas for $D$ and $\bar D$, the only nonzero mixed terms in $\{D_\alpha,\bar D_{\dot\beta}\}$ are $\{\partial^L_{\theta^\alpha},-i\theta^\gamma\sigma^\mu_{\gamma\dot\beta}\partial_\mu\}=-i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\partial_\mu$ and $\{i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\gamma}\bar\theta^{\dot\gamma}\partial_\mu,-\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\beta}}\}=-i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\partial_\mu$. Their sum is the stated mixed anticommutator. In each same-type anticommutator, the derivative-only terms vanish by [F2], the terms with one coordinate and one derivative cancel or are zero because the differentiated coordinate is of the other type, and the products of two identical-type odd coordinates vanish after symmetrization. Thus both same-type anticommutators are zero. [F1, F2, F3, algebra]

1.2 With $y=x+i\theta\sigma\bar\theta$, the left derivative gives $\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}y^\mu=-i\theta^\beta\sigma^\mu_{\beta\dot\alpha}$: the minus sign comes from differentiating the rightmost odd factor past the left odd $\theta^\beta$. The finite Taylor definition and ordinary algebra therefore give, on functions expressed in $(y,\theta,\bar\theta)$, $\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}|_x=\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}|_y-i\theta^\beta\sigma^\mu_{\beta\dot\alpha}\partial_{y^\mu}$ and $\partial_{x^\mu}=\partial_{y^\mu}$. Substitution into $\bar D$ cancels the two $\theta\sigma\partial_y$ terms, leaving $\bar D_{\dot\alpha}=-\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}|_y$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]

1.3 Expand a function in the unique ordered basis of $\Lambda_4$ with coefficients in $C^\infty(\mathbb R^{1,3},\mathcal A)$. In $(y,\theta,\bar\theta)$ coordinates, $\bar D_{\dot\alpha}\Phi=0$ for both dotted indices means both left derivatives in the barred generators vanish. For a fixed generator, distinct basis monomials containing it map under its derivative to distinct basis monomials not containing it, with coefficients $+1$ or $-1$; hence its derivative has zero kernel exactly on the span of monomials not containing it. Applying this to both barred generators shows $\Phi$ is independent of $\bar\theta$. The remaining ordered basis is $1,\theta^1,\theta^2,\theta^1\theta^2$, so its coefficients are unique and give the four displayed component functions and parities. [F1, F2, step 1.2, basis]

2.1 Direct expansion of $Q_\alpha$ against $\bar D_{\dot\beta}$ gives two opposite terms: $\{\partial^L_{\theta^\alpha},-i\theta^\gamma\sigma^\mu_{\gamma\dot\beta}\partial_\mu\}=-i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\partial_\mu$ and $\{-i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\gamma}\bar\theta^{\dot\gamma}\partial_\mu,-\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\beta}}\}=+i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\partial_\mu$, so $\{Q_\alpha,\bar D_{\dot\beta}\}=0$. All other terms vanish by the same independent-generator and odd-square identities. The two barred operators $\bar Q_{\dot\alpha}$ and $\bar D_{\dot\beta}$ also anticommute: their barred derivative parts anticommute, their $\theta\partial_x$ parts anticommute, and each barred derivative anticommutes with each $\theta$ factor because it differentiates a different generator. Therefore $\bar D_{\dot\beta}(\delta_{\epsilon,\bar\epsilon}\Phi)=0$ whenever $\bar D_{\dot\beta}\Phi=0$, accounting for the odd parameter crossing the odd derivative. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]

