2.1 Since $s(\Lambda_g^\vee p)=s(p)$, Lorentz covariance of $E$ implies $U(g,0)F(A)U(g,0)^*=F(A)$. Translation unitaries are bounded Borel functions of $E$, so they commute with every $F(A)$; $\Gamma$ commutes with $E$ by [F1]. Hence $\operatorname{ran}F(A)$ is closed and graded, invariant under the integrated even group, and invariant under every charge and adjoint with dense restricted domains: density follows by applying the bounded projection $F(A)$ to a dense operator domain. Irreducibility as defined in [F1] forces $F(A)=0$ or $F(A)=I$. [F1, step 1.1, algebra]

2.2 Choose any nonzero vector and normalize it to a unit vector $\psi\in\mathcal H$; set $\mu(A)=\langle F(A)\psi,\psi\rangle$. By [F3] this is a countably additive probability measure, and by step 2.1 every value is $0$ or $1$. The set $R=\{r\ge0:\mu([0,r])=1\}$ is nonempty: otherwise the countable union $[0,\infty)=\bigcup_{n\ge1}[0,n]$ would have measure zero. Put $m^2=\inf R$, which is a finite nonnegative real. [A1, F1, F3, step 2.1, choose]

2.3 For every $\varepsilon>0$, the infimum property gives $r\in R$ with $r<m^2+\varepsilon$, so $\mu([0,m^2+\varepsilon])=1$. If $m^2>\varepsilon$, then $m^2-\varepsilon<m^2$ is not in $R$, and the zero-or-one property gives $\mu([0,m^2-\varepsilon])=0$. Thus for all sufficiently large $n$, the nested intervals $I_n=(\max(0,m^2-1/n),m^2+1/n]$ have measure one; when $m^2=0$, use $I_n=[0,1/n]$. Countable additivity of the finite measure gives continuity from above, hence $\mu(\{m^2\})=\lim_n\mu(I_n)=1$. Therefore $F(\{m^2\})\ne0$, and step 2.1 forces $F(\{m^2\})=I$. If $A$ contains $m^2$, monotonicity gives $F(A)=I$; if not, then $\mu(A)=0$ because $A$ is disjoint from $\{m^2\}$, so the zero-or-one property gives $F(A)=0$. This rule also proves uniqueness: any second value with projection $I$ would have to equal $m^2$, since the singleton projections for distinct values are orthogonal. [A1, F1, F3, step 2.1, step 2.2]

3.1 By [F4], $m^2$ has a unique nonnegative square root $m$. Step 1.2 shows that each charge maps vectors in a fixed mass-squared subspace into that same subspace; step 2.3 shows there is only one such spectral value in the irreducible representation. The argument uses the pushforward PVM rather than a normalizable sharp-momentum vector, and it includes $m=0$ without introducing a rest frame. [F4, step 1.2, step 2.3]

