Pure-point graded trace-class index data: [[def-qftx-graded-trace-class-index-data]].

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The graded Hilbert direct-sum decomposition, odd self-adjoint supercharge, supplied positive-energy block unitaries, compatible Hilbert basis, and trace-class heat operators for all $\beta>0$ in the index-data definition.

[F1] [[def-qftx-graded-trace-class-index-data]]: each positive-energy block has finite-dimensional parity subspaces $\mathcal H_\lambda^\pm$ and a supplied unitary $U_\lambda:\mathcal H_\lambda^+\to\mathcal H_\lambda^-$. 

[F2] [[def-qftx-graded-trace-class-index-data]]: the heat operator is diagonal with eigenvalue $1$ on the zero-energy basis and $e^{-\beta\lambda}$ on each parity subspace at energy $\lambda>0$; it is trace class, so its diagonal family in the supplied Hilbert basis is absolutely summable.

## Proof

**Proof technique:** blockwise supersymmetric pairing.

**Given:** [F1]–[F2] and $\beta>0$.

1.1 Since $U_\lambda$ is a linear bijection between finite-dimensional spaces, $m_\lambda^+:=\dim\mathcal H_\lambda^+=\dim\mathcal H_\lambda^-=:m_\lambda^-$ for every $\lambda\in\Sigma$. On any supplied orthonormal basis of $\mathcal H_\lambda^\pm$, [F2] gives $\langle T_\beta e,e\rangle=e^{-\beta\lambda}$ for each basis vector. Therefore the positive-energy contribution to the signed basis sum at this fixed $\lambda$ is $e^{-\beta\lambda}(m_\lambda^+-m_\lambda^-)=0$. [A1, F1, F2, algebra]

1.2 By [F2], the full diagonal family is absolutely summable. For the partition into its energy blocks, the absolute value of each block sum is at most the sum of the absolute values in that block; hence the family of block sums is also absolutely summable. To see that its sum equals the original sum, fix $\varepsilon>0$ and choose a finite set of basis vectors whose omitted absolute tail is below $\varepsilon$. Only finitely many energy blocks meet that finite set, and the omitted absolute tail bounds the difference between the full sum and the sum of those complete block sums. Letting $\varepsilon$ decrease to zero proves that grouping by energy preserves the sum. Step 1.1 cancels every positive-energy block; each zero-energy even basis vector contributes $1$ and each zero-energy odd basis vector contributes $-1$. The remaining finite sum is $m_0^+-m_0^-$, independent of $\beta$. The proof uses no trace cyclicity and no positive-energy eigenvector outside the given discrete decomposition. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎

