    - title: \"S. J. Gates Jr., M. T. Grisaru, M. Roček and W. Siegel, Superspace, or One Thousand and One Lessons in Supersymmetry\"
      url: \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0108200\"
      locator: \"§8.4, supertrace and index context; the positive-energy eigenspace map is proved locally from the stated domains.\"
verification:
  precheck: pending
---

## Statement

Assume Countable Choice. Let "'$'"\\mathcal H=\\mathcal H"'^+'"\\oplus\\mathcal H"'^-$ be a complex Hilbert space with grading operator $'"\\Gamma=+I"'$ on $'"\\mathcal H"'^+$ and $'"\\Gamma=-I"'$ on $'"\\mathcal H"'^-$. Let $Q$ be a densely defined self-adjoint operator with domain invariant under $'"\\Gamma"'$ and $Q'"\\Gamma\\psi=-\\Gamma Q\\psi"'$ for every $'"\\psi\\in D(Q)"'$. Set $H=Q''^2$ on the composition domain $D(H)='"\\{\\psi\\in D(Q):Q\\psi\\in D(Q)\\}"'$. For each $'"\\lambda>0"'$, let $E_'"\\lambda=\\{\\psi\\in D(H):H\\psi=\\lambda\\psi\\}"'$ be finite-dimensional, and put $E_'"\\lambda"'^'"\\pm=E_\\lambda\\cap\\mathcal H"'^'"\\pm"'$. Then $Q$ maps $E_'"\\lambda"'$ into itself, and

$$U_'"\\lambda:=\\lambda"'^{-1/2}Q|_{E_'"\\lambda"'^+}:E_'"\\lambda"'^+'"\\longrightarrow E_\\lambda"'^-$$

is a unitary isomorphism, with inverse $'"\\lambda"'^{-1/2}Q|_{E_'"\\lambda"'^-}$. In particular $'"\\dim E_\\lambda"'^+='"\\dim E_\\lambda"'^-$. No discrete spectrum or finite-multiplicity assumption is made about energies other than the selected $'"\\lambda"'$.

## Statement references

[Martin, §7.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9709356v7).

[Gates et al., §8.4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0108200).

Hilbert space: [[def-hilbert-space]].

Real and complex inner-product spaces and their induced length: [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]].

Unbounded linear operators: domain, graph and extension: [[def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph]].

Adjoint of a densely defined operator: [[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]].

Symmetric, self-adjoint and essentially self-adjoint operators: [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]].

Every nonnegative real number has a unique nonnegative square root: [[thm-of-square-roots]].

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** $'"\\mathcal H"'$, $'"\\Gamma"'$, $Q$, the composition operator $H=Q''^2$, and a finite-dimensional positive-energy eigenspace $E_'"\\lambda"'$ as in the Statement. Assume Countable Choice for the imported adjoint and self-adjoint definitions.

[A1] Countable Choice is used by the imported unbounded adjoint and self-adjoint operator definitions. [[def-countable-choice]]

[F1] [[def-hilbert-space]]: $'"\\mathcal H"'$ is complete in the norm induced by its complex inner product.

[F2] [[def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph]]: the domain of a composition $Q''^2$ is $'"\\{\\psi\\in D(Q):Q\\psi\\in D(Q)\\}"'$.

[F3] [[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]]: self-adjointness gives $'"\\langle Qx,y\\rangle=\\langle x,Qy\\rangle"'$ whenever $x,y'"\\in D(Q)"'$.

[F4] [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]: a densely defined operator is self-adjoint when its domain agrees with the domain of its actual adjoint and the values agree.

[F5] [[thm-of-square-roots]]: every positive real $'"\\lambda"'$ has a unique positive square root $'"\\sqrt\\lambda"'$.

## Proof

**Proof technique:** operator-domain calculation.

**Given:** [A1], [F1]–[F5], and the domain, grading and anticommutation hypotheses in the Statement.

1.1 If $'"\\psi\\in E_\\lambda"'$, then $'"\\psi\\in D(Q)"'$, $Q'"\\psi\\in D(Q)"'$ and $Q''^2'"\\psi=\\lambda\\psi"'$ by [F2]. Thus $Q'"\\psi\\in D(Q)"'$ and $Q(Q'"\\psi)=\\lambda\\psi\\in D(Q)"'$, so a second application of [F2] gives $Q'"\\psi\\in D(H)"'$. Moreover $H(Q'"\\psi)=Q(Q"'^2'"\\psi)=Q(\\lambda\\psi)=\\lambda Q\\psi"'$. Therefore $Q$ maps $E_'"\\lambda"'$ into itself. [F2, given, algebra]

1.2 The domain of $H$ is invariant under $'"\\Gamma"'$: if $'"\\psi\\in D(H)"'$, then $'"\\Gamma\\psi\\in D(Q)"'$ by hypothesis and $Q'"\\Gamma\\psi=-\\Gamma Q\\psi\\in D(Q)"'$ because $Q'"\\psi\\in D(Q)"'$ and $D(Q)$ is $'"\\Gamma"'$-invariant. Applying the anticommutation relation twice gives $H'"\\Gamma\\psi=\\Gamma H\\psi"'$. Hence $'"\\Gamma E_\\lambda=E_\\lambda"'$, and $Q'"\\Gamma=-\\Gamma Q"'$ shows that $Q$ maps $E_'"\\lambda"'^+$ to $E_'"\\lambda"'^-$ and $E_'"\\lambda"'^-$ to $E_'"\\lambda"'^+$. [given, F2, step 1.1, algebra]

1.3 For $'"\\psi,\\varphi\\in E_\\lambda"'$, both vectors lie in $D(Q)$ and $Q'"\\psi\\in D(Q)"'$, so self-adjointness and [F3] give $'"\\langle Q\\psi,Q\\varphi\\rangle=\\langle Q"'^2'"\\psi,\\varphi\\rangle=\\lambda\\langle\\psi,\\varphi\\rangle"'$. By [F5], division by $'"\\sqrt\\lambda"'$ therefore makes $Q$ an isometry on $E_'"\\lambda"'$. Also $(Q/'"\\sqrt\\lambda)"'^2'"\\psi=Q"'^2'"\\psi/\\lambda=\\psi"'$ there. Together with the parity exchange in step 1.2, this proves that the restriction $U_'"\\lambda:E_\\lambda"'^+'"\\to E_\\lambda"'^-$ is an isometry with inverse the restriction from $E_'"\\lambda"'^-$ to $E_'"\\lambda"'^+$; it is consequently unitary and the finite dimensions agree. [A1, F3, F4, F5, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
EOF
