Symmetric, self-adjoint and essentially self-adjoint operators: [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]].

Every nonnegative real number has a unique nonnegative square root: [[thm-of-square-roots]].

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** $\mathcal H$, $\Gamma$, $Q$, the composition operator $H=Q^2$, and a finite-dimensional positive-energy eigenspace $E_\lambda$ as in the Statement. Assume Countable Choice for the imported adjoint and self-adjoint definitions.

[A1] Countable Choice is used by the imported unbounded adjoint and self-adjoint operator definitions. [[def-countable-choice]]

[F1] [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]: the complex inner product is linear in its first argument, conjugate-linear in its second and positive definite.

[F2] [[def-hilbert-space]]: $\mathcal H$ is complete in the norm induced by its complex inner product.

[F3] [[def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph]]: the domain of a composition $Q^2$ is $\{\psi\in D(Q):Q\psi\in D(Q)\}$.

[F4] [[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]]: self-adjointness gives $\langle Qx,y\rangle=\langle x,Qy\rangle$ whenever $x,y\in D(Q)$.

[F5] [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]: a densely defined operator is self-adjoint when its domain agrees with the domain of its actual adjoint and the values agree.

[F6] [[thm-of-square-roots]]: every positive real $\lambda$ has a unique positive square root $\sqrt\lambda$.

## Proof

**Proof technique:** operator-domain calculation.

**Given:** [A1], [F1]–[F5], and the domain, grading and anticommutation hypotheses in the Statement.

1.1 If $\psi\in E_\lambda$, then $\psi\in D(Q)$, $Q\psi\in D(Q)$ and $Q^2\psi=\lambda\psi$ by [F2]. Thus $Q\psi\in D(Q)$ and $Q(Q\psi)=\lambda\psi\in D(Q)$, so a second application of [F2] gives $Q\psi\in D(H)$. Moreover $H(Q\psi)=Q(Q^2\psi)=Q(\lambda\psi)=\lambda Q\psi$. Therefore $Q$ maps $E_\lambda$ into itself. [F2, F3, given, algebra]

1.2 The domain of $H$ is invariant under $\Gamma$: if $\psi\in D(H)$, then $\Gamma\psi\in D(Q)$ by hypothesis and $Q\Gamma\psi=-\Gamma Q\psi\in D(Q)$ because $Q\psi\in D(Q)$ and $D(Q)$ is $\Gamma$-invariant. Applying the anticommutation relation twice gives $H\Gamma\psi=\Gamma H\psi$. Hence $\Gamma E_\lambda=E_\lambda$, and $Q\Gamma=-\Gamma Q$ shows that $Q$ maps $E_\lambda^+$ to $E_\lambda^-$ and $E_\lambda^-$ to $E_\lambda^+$. [given, F3, step 1.1, algebra]

1.3 For $\psi,\varphi\in E_\lambda, both vectors lie in $D(Q)$ and $Q\psi\in D(Q)$, so self-adjointness and [F3] give $\langle Q\psi,Q\varphi\rangle=\langle Q^2\psi,\varphi\rangle=\lambda\langle\psi,\varphi\rangle$. By [F5], division by $\sqrt\lambda$ therefore makes $Q$ an isometry on $E_\lambda$. Also $(Q/\sqrt\lambda)^2\psi=Q^2\psi/\lambda=\psi$ there. Together with the parity exchange in step 1.2, this proves that the restriction $U_\lambda:E_\lambda^+\to E_\lambda^-$ is an isometry with inverse the restriction from $E_\lambda^-$ to $E_\lambda^+$; it is consequently unitary and the finite dimensions agree. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]

2.1 If $\psi\in\ker H$, then $\psi\in D(Q^2)$, so $Q\psi\in D(Q)$. By self-adjointness and [F4], $\|Q\psi\|^2=\langle Q\psi,Q\psi\rangle=\langle Q^2\psi,\psi\rangle=0$. Positive definiteness in [F1] forces $Q\psi=0$. [F1, F2, F4, given, algebra] ∎

