  | **Given:** An integer $N\ge1$, the finite-dimensional graded spaces, bracket table, adjoint operation and Pauli matrices specified above.
  | 
  | [F1] [[def-qftx-super-poincare-algebra-4d]]: $\mathfrak g_{\bar0}=\mathfrak l\oplus V\oplus C_N$, $\mathfrak g_{\bar1}=(S\otimes\mathbb C^N)\oplus(\dot S\otimes(\mathbb C^N)^*)$; $\mathfrak l$ acts by matrix commutator, tensor action and the fundamental actions; $V$ and $C_N$ are abelian, $C_N$ is central, and $V$ acts trivially on the odd part; the three odd brackets and the star operation are exactly those in the Statement.
  | 
  | [F2] [[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]]: $\mathbb C$ is a field with the coordinate addition and multiplication displayed in its Statement.
  | 
  | [F3] [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]: if $z=a+bi$ then $\overline z=a-bi$; conjugation is conjugate-linear and involutive.
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Proof technique:** direct.
  | 
  | **Given:** The finite-dimensional graded space and operations in [F1], with the scalar field and conjugation in [F2]–[F3].
  | 
  | 1.1 The matrix commutators are antisymmetric and the odd-odd brackets are symmetric under interchange of the full generator labels: for the same-chirality bracket, $\epsilon_{\beta\alpha}Z^{JI}=(-\epsilon_{\alpha\beta})(-Z^{IJ})=\epsilon_{\alpha\beta}Z^{IJ}$, and the dotted case is identical. The mixed bracket is symmetric by its Kronecker delta and the graded-skew rule. Each listed bracket has parity equal to the sum of its input parities, so the bracket is degree-preserving and graded-skew. [F1, algebra]
  | 
  | 1.2 For matrices $X,Y,Z$, both $X(YZ-ZY)-(YZ-ZY)X$ and $(XY-YX)Z-Z(XY-YX)+Y(XZ-ZX)-(XZ-ZX)Y$ expand to $XYZ-XZY-YZX+ZYX$, so the commutator on $\mathfrak l$ satisfies Jacobi. Write $\rho_V(A,B)=A\otimes I+I\otimes B$. Operators acting on different tensor factors commute, hence $[\rho_V(A,B),\rho_V(A',B')]=[A,A']\otimes I+I\otimes[B,B']=\rho_V([(A,B),(A',B')])$. The two fundamental odd actions are representations by the same matrix-commutator identity, and the actions on $C_N$ and by $V$ are zero. Thus $\mathfrak l$ acts by representations on the abelian space $V\oplus C_N$ and on $\mathfrak g_{\bar1}$; expanding the even Jacobi identity leaves precisely these representation identities, while brackets internal to $V\oplus C_N$ vanish. [F1, F2, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 For two even inputs and one odd input, the Jacobi expression is $[\rho(L),\rho(L')]q-\rho([L,L'])q$, which is zero by the fundamental-action identities in [F1]; if either even input lies in $V\oplus C_N$, its odd action is zero and the same expression vanishes. This checks all orderings of the two-even/one-odd case by graded skew-symmetry. [F1, step 1.2]
  | 
  | 2.2 For one even input $L=(A,B)$ and two odd inputs, Jacobi is equivariance of their odd bracket. On a mixed pair, the two fundamental actions on $Q$ and $\bar Q$ give exactly $A\otimes I+I\otimes B$ on $T$, so the mixed bracket is equivariant. On an equal-chirality pair, writing $A=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&-a\end{pmatrix}$ gives $A^{\mathsf T}\epsilon+\epsilon A=0$ by direct multiplication; the identical calculation for $B$ preserves the dotted alternating form. The flavor tensors $Z^{IJ}$ and $\bar Z_{IJ}$ are Lorentz scalars, so both central brackets are equivariant. Even inputs from $V\oplus C_N$ act trivially on each side. [F1, step 1.2, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.3 The given star squares to the identity on each generator. On the matrix-commutator bracket, entrywise conjugation preserves matrix multiplication and the minus signs in $(A,B)^*$ reverse the bracket; on the fundamental and tensor actions, star exchanges the two spinor factors and the corresponding minus sign gives $[x,y]^*=-[x^*,y^*]$. On odd-odd brackets, star exchanges $Q$ with $\bar Q$, transposes the bispinor indices of $T$, and exchanges $Z$ with $\bar Z$; the real alternating-form coefficients and antisymmetric flavor labels give $[x,y]^*=[x^*,y^*]$, as required for two odd inputs. Zero brackets remain zero. For homogeneous even-even, even-odd and odd-odd inputs, substituting these identities into $\tau=-*$, $i*$ and $-*$ respectively gives $\tau([x,y])=[\tau x,\tau y]$; conjugate-linearity gives $\tau^2=I$. Therefore the fixed space is closed under the bracket. Every $x$ decomposes as $x_+ + i x_-$ with $x_+=(x+\tau x)/2$ and $x_-=(x-\tau x)/(2i)$ fixed by $\tau$, so this fixed real algebra complexifies to the displayed graded algebra. [F1, F3, step 1.1, step 1.2]
  | 
  | 3.1 For three odd inputs, each inner bracket lies in $V\oplus C_N$. Both summands act trivially on the odd space, so each of the three terms in the odd-odd-odd super-Jacobi identity is zero. The four parity patterns checked in the preceding cases exhaust homogeneous triples, and multilinearity gives the identity for arbitrary triples. [F1, step 2.1, step 2.2]
  | 
  | 4.1 The Pauli matrices satisfy $\sigma_i\sigma_j+\sigma_j\sigma_i=2\delta_{ij}I_2$ and $\operatorname{tr}(\sigma_i)=0$. For $\mu=\nu=0$ the sigma identity is $2I_2=-2\eta^{00}I_2$; for one temporal and one spatial index the two terms cancel; for spatial $i,j$ their sum is $-(\sigma_i\sigma_j+\sigma_j\sigma_i)=-2\delta_{ij}I_2$. Also $(p_0I_2+p_i\sigma_i)^2=(p_0^2+|\vec p|^2)I_2+2p_0p_i\sigma_i$ and direct $2\times2$ determinant expansion gives $\det(p_0I_2+p_i\sigma_i)=p_0^2-|\vec p|^2=-\eta^{\mu\nu}p_\mu p_\nu$. With $P_0=-M$ and $P_i=0$, the mixed bracket is therefore $-2\sigma^0P_0=2MI_2$. [F1, F2, step 1.1] ∎
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | The lemma verifies a algebraic construction. It proves neither the existence of an interacting quantum field theory nor the existence, stability or empirical observation of a supersymmetric particle state.
REPAIR items/thm-qftx-supermultiplet-mass-degeneracy.md: passes only after auto-repair — adopt the canonical form:
  | **Given:** An irreducible positive unitary super-Poincare representation as in the Statement, and its joint momentum PVM $E$.
  | 
  | [F1] [[def-qftx-unitary-super-poincare-representation]]: $E$ is a joint PVM with future-cone support, it is covariant under the even Lorentz group, it commutes with translations, and every charge and adjoint has the stated reducing-projection/domain property for each $E(B)$.
  | 
  | [F2] [[def-projection-valued-measure]]: each $E(B)$ is an orthogonal projection and $E$ is strongly countably additive on disjoint Borel sets.
  | 
  | [F3] [[lem-scalar-and-complex-measures-from-a-pvm]]: for any unit vector $\psi$, $\mu_\psi(A)=\langle F(A)\psi,\psi\rangle$ is a countably additive probability measure once $F$ is a normalized PVM.
  | 
  | [F4] [[thm-of-square-roots]]: every nonnegative real number has a unique nonnegative square root.
  | 
  | [A1] Assume Countable Choice, as required by the PVM and scalar-measure suppliers used here ([[def-countable-choice]]).
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Proof technique:** direct.
  | 
  | **Given:** $\mathcal H$, $U$, $\Gamma$, the charge operators, and $E$ as in [F1].
  | 
  | 1.1 The function $s(p)=-\eta^{\mu\nu}p_\mu p_\nu=(p^0)^2-|\vec p|^2$ is continuous and nonnegative on the closed future cone in [F1]. For each Borel $A\subseteq[0,\infty)$, $\{p\in\mathcal C_+^*:s(p)\in A\}$ is Borel in the full covector space because $\mathcal C_+^*$ is closed and $s$ is continuous there. Inverse images preserve disjoint unions, and the preimage of $[0,\infty)$ is $\mathcal C_+^*$, whose projection is $I$ by [F1]; therefore the pushforward $F(A):=E(\{p\in\mathcal C_+^*:s(p)\in A\})$ is a normalized PVM on $[0,\infty)$ by strong countable additivity of $E$. [A1, F1, F2, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 For each Borel $A$, $F(A)$ is the projection $E(\{p\in\mathcal C_+^*:s(p)\in A\})$. The charge-domain reduction in [F1] therefore gives $F(A)D(Q)\subseteq D(Q)$ and $QF(A)\psi=F(A)Q\psi$ for every $\psi\in D(Q)$; the same argument applies to $Q^*$. This proves the spectral-subspace assertion. [F1, step 1.1]
  | 
  | 2.2 Since $s(\Lambda_g^\vee p)=s(p)$, Lorentz covariance of $E$ implies $U(g,0)F(A)U(g,0)^*=F(A)$. Translation unitaries are bounded Borel functions of $E$, so they commute with every $F(A)$; $\Gamma$ commutes with $E$ by [F1]. Hence $\operatorname{ran}F(A)$ is closed and graded, invariant under the integrated even group, and invariant under every charge and adjoint with dense restricted domains: density follows by applying the bounded projection $F(A)$ to a dense operator domain. Irreducibility as defined in [F1] forces $F(A)=0$ or $F(A)=I$. [F1, step 1.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 3.1 Choose any nonzero vector and normalize it to a unit vector $\psi\in\mathcal H$; set $\mu(A)=\langle F(A)\psi,\psi\rangle$. By [F3] this is a countably additive probability measure, and by step 2.2 every value is $0$ or $1$. The set $R=\{r\ge0:\mu([0,r])=1\}$ is nonempty: otherwise the countable union $[0,\infty)=\bigcup_{n\ge1}[0,n]$ would have measure zero. Put $m^2=\inf R$, which is a finite nonnegative real. [A1, F1, F3, step 2.2, choose]
  | 
  | 4.1 For every $\varepsilon>0$, the infimum property gives $r\in R$ with $r<m^2+\varepsilon$, so $\mu([0,m^2+\varepsilon])=1$. If $m^2>\varepsilon$, then $m^2-\varepsilon<m^2$ is not in $R$, and the zero-or-one property gives $\mu([0,m^2-\varepsilon])=0$. Thus for all sufficiently large $n$, the nested intervals $I_n=(\max(0,m^2-1/n),m^2+1/n]$ have measure one; when $m^2=0$, use $I_n=[0,1/n]$. Countable additivity of the finite measure gives continuity from above, hence $\mu(\{m^2\})=\lim_n\mu(I_n)=1$. Therefore $F(\{m^2\})\ne0$, and step 2.2 forces $F(\{m^2\})=I$. If $A$ contains $m^2$, monotonicity gives $F(A)=I$; if not, then $\mu(A)=0$ because $A$ is disjoint from $\{m^2\}$, so the zero-or-one property gives $F(A)=0$. This rule also proves uniqueness: any second value with projection $I$ would have to equal $m^2$, since the singleton projections for distinct values are orthogonal. [A1, F1, F3, step 2.2, step 3.1]
  | 
  | 5.1 By [F4], $m^2$ has a unique nonnegative square root $m$. Step 2.1 shows that each charge maps vectors in a fixed mass-squared subspace into that same subspace; step 4.1 shows there is only one such spectral value in the irreducible representation. The argument uses the pushforward PVM rather than a normalizable sharp-momentum vector, and it includes $m=0$ without introducing a rest frame. [F4, step 2.1, step 4.1]
  | 
  | 6.1 If an additional unitary group $V(g)$ preserves $D(Q)$ and satisfies $V(g)Q\psi=QV(g)\psi$ there, then for every $\psi\in D(Q)$ one has $V(g)(Q\psi)=Q(V(g)\psi)$; this is exactly the intertwining relation. Without that additional commutation hypothesis no gauge representation equality follows. [given, algebra] ∎
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | The theorem fixes the invariant-mass support of an irreducible representation and the mass sector of every nonzero supercharge image. It asserts no multiplicity formula and no physical particle existence or stability result.
REPAIR items/thm-qftx-n-extended-central-charge-bounds.md: passes only after auto-repair — adopt the canonical form:
  | **Given:** $N\ge2$, $M>0$, a nonzero rest-fiber Hilbert space $\mathcal K$, scalar central-charge matrix $Z$, and common invariant product domain $\mathcal D$ as in the Statement.
  | 
  | [A1] Assume Countable Choice, as required by the PVM and unbounded-operator suppliers used by the representation definition ([[def-countable-choice]]).
  | 
  | [F1] [[def-qftx-super-poincare-algebra-4d]]: $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\epsilon_{12}=1$, $\{Q^I_\alpha,\bar Q_{\dot\beta J}\}=-2\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}P_\mu\delta^I{}_J$, $\{Q^I_\alpha,Q^J_\beta\}=2\epsilon_{\alpha\beta}Z^{IJ}$, and the scalar central-charge matrix is antisymmetric in $I,J$.
  | 
  | [F2] [[def-qftx-unitary-super-poincare-representation]]: the Hilbert inner product is positive, $\bar Q=(Q)^*$ is the actual adjoint with its actual domain, and every product in these anticommutators is defined on the common invariant domain.
  | 
  | [F3] [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]: for $z=a+bi$, $\bar z=a-bi$ and $|z|=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$.
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Proof technique:** direct.
  | 
  | **Given:** The algebra and adjoint representation in [F1]–[F2], restricted to the fixed rest fiber in the Statement.
  | 
  | 1.1 At rest $P_0=-M I$ and $P_j=0$, so [F1] gives $\{Q^I_1,(Q^J_1)^*\}=2M\delta_{IJ}I$ and $\{Q^I_2,(Q^J_2)^*\}=2M\delta_{IJ}I$. Put $q_I=Q^I_1$ and $r_I=(Q^I_2)^*$; then $\{q_I,r_J^*\}=2z^{IJ}I$ and $\{r_J,q_I^*\}=2\bar z_{IJ}I$, with all these products defined on $\mathcal D$. [A1, F1, F2, given]
  | 
  | 2.1 For $u,v\in\mathbb C^N$, define on $\mathcal D$ the charge combination $B_{u,v}=\sum_I\bar u_Iq_I+\sum_I\bar v_Ir_I$ and the combination of its summand adjoints $B^\sharp_{u,v}=\sum_Iu_Iq_I^*+\sum_Iv_Ir_I^*$. Expanding the algebraic anticommutator on $\mathcal D$ gives $B_{u,v}B^\sharp_{u,v}+B^\sharp_{u,v}B_{u,v}=\left(2M(\|u\|_2^2+\|v\|_2^2)+4\operatorname{Re}(u^*Zv)\right)I$. The diagonal blocks use the two mixed rest anticommutators in step 1.1, and the off-diagonal terms are $2u^*Zv$ and its complex conjugate. For every $\psi\in\mathcal D$, expanding the quadratic form term by term and using the adjoint identity for each individual charge gives $\langle(B_{u,v}B^\sharp_{u,v}+B^\sharp_{u,v}B_{u,v})\psi,\psi\rangle=\|B_{u,v}\psi\|^2+\|B^\sharp_{u,v}\psi\|^2\ge0$. [F2, step 1.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 3.1 Fix a unit vector $v\in\mathbb C^N$. If $Zv\ne0$, choose $u=-Zv/\|Zv\|_2$, so $\|u\|_2=1$ and $u^*Zv=-\|Zv\|_2$. Applying step 2.1 to any nonzero $\psi\in\mathcal D$ yields $0\le4(M-\|Zv\|_2)\|\psi\|^2$, hence $M\ge\|Zv\|_2$. If $Zv=0$, the same inequality follows from $M>0$. Taking the supremum over all unit $v$ proves $M\ge\|Z\|_{\mathrm{op}}$. [given, step 2.1, algebra, choose]
  | 
  | 4.1 When $N=2$, $Z=\begin{pmatrix}0&z\\-z&0\end{pmatrix}$, so direct multiplication gives $Z^*Z=|z|^2I_2$ and therefore $\|Zv\|_2=|z|\|v\|_2$ for every $v$; thus $\|Z\|_{\mathrm{op}}=|z|$. With $q=Q^1_1$ and $r=Q^2_2$, expanding $A=q-e^{i\phi}r^*$ and $A^\sharp=q^*-e^{-i\phi}r$ gives $AA^\sharp+A^\sharp A=\{q,q^*\}+\{r^*,r\}-e^{-i\phi}\{q,r\}-e^{i\phi}\{r^*,q^*\}=2M+2M-2e^{-i\phi}z-2e^{i\phi}\bar z=4(M-|z|)I$ on $\mathcal D$. This proves the stated N=2 identity with the declared phase convention. [F1, F3, step 1.1, step 3.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 5.1 If $M=|z|$, step 4.1 gives $AA^\sharp+A^\sharp A=0$ on $\mathcal D$. For every $\psi\in\mathcal D$, the actual-adjoint identity for the individual charge summands in [F2] gives $0=\langle(AA^\sharp+A^\sharp A)\psi,\psi\rangle=\|A\psi\|^2+\|A^\sharp\psi\|^2$. Each squared norm is nonnegative, so both vanish. If $\mathcal K$ is finite-dimensional, $A^\sharp=A^*$ and this is the ordinary operator-adjoint conclusion; in general it is the stated dense-domain conclusion. The result is confined to the fixed rest fiber and asserts no state existence or stability. [F2, step 4.1] ∎
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | For general $N$, the norm inequality does not by itself claim a multiplicity formula or a shortened representation. Those require additional representation-theoretic hypotheses and proofs.
REPAIR items/lem-qftx-grassmann-coefficient-calculus.md: passes only after auto-repair — adopt the canonical form:
  | **Given:** The finite basis $e_S$, multiplication sign $\nu(S,T)$, coefficient superalgebra, derivative actions and ordered measures in the Statement.
  | 
  | [F1] [[def-qftx-superspace-grassmann-algebra]]: the basis is indexed by all subsets of $\{1,2,3,4\}$, $e_Se_T=0$ on overlap and otherwise equals $(-1)^{\nu(S,T)}e_{S\cup T}$; coefficient multiplication is the graded tensor product, and the left/right derivative actions on homogeneous coefficient-left tensors are $(-1)^{|a|}a\otimes\partial^L e_S$ and $a\otimes\partial^R e_S$, respectively.
  | 
  | [F2] [[def-qftx-superspace-grassmann-algebra]]: for $i\in S$, $\partial^L_{\xi_i}e_S=(-1)^{|\{s\in S:s<i\}|}e_{S\setminus\{i\}}$ and $\partial^R_{\xi_i}e_S=(-1)^{|\{s\in S:s>i\}|}e_{S\setminus\{i\}}$, and both vanish when $i\notin S$.
  | 
  | [F3] [[def-qftx-superspace-grassmann-algebra]]: the one-variable integral extracts the coefficient with that variable ordered first; the four-variable measure is $\int d\bar\theta^{\dot2}d\bar\theta^{\dot1}d\theta^2d\theta^1$.
  | 
  | [F4] [[def-qftx-superspace-grassmann-algebra]]: conjugation reverses product order and exchanges $\theta^\alpha$ with $\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}$.
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Proof technique:** direct.
  | 
  | **Given:** The finite basis and signed product in [F1], and the specified derivative, integral and conjugation operations in [F2]–[F4].
  | 
  | 1.1 The element $e_\varnothing$ is a two-sided identity because $\nu(\varnothing,S)=\nu(S,\varnothing)=0$. For $S,T,U\subseteq\{1,2,3,4\}$, if any two sets overlap, both parenthesizations $(e_Se_T)e_U$ and $e_S(e_Te_U)$ vanish: if the first pair overlaps one side vanishes immediately and the other retains that common generator, and the same argument applies to the other pairs. If $S,T,U$ are pairwise disjoint, the left sign exponent is $\nu(S,T)+\nu(S\cup T,U)=\nu(S,T)+\nu(S,U)+\nu(T,U)$, while the right exponent is $\nu(T,U)+\nu(S,T\cup U)=\nu(T,U)+\nu(S,T)+\nu(S,U)$. The signs agree, proving associativity on basis elements and hence by trilinearity on all elements. [F1, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 If $S\cap T\ne\varnothing$, both products vanish. If the sets are disjoint, every ordered pair in $S\times T$ has exactly one of $s>t$ or $t>s$, so $\nu(S,T)+\nu(T,S)=|S||T|$. Thus $e_Se_T=(-1)^{|S||T|}e_Te_S$. This proves supercommutativity. [F1, step 1.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.2 Put $a=|\{s\in S:s<i\}|$, $c=|\{s\in S:s>i\}|$, $b=|\{t\in T:t<i\}|$, and $d=|\{t\in T:t>i\}|$. If $S$ and $T$ are disjoint and $i\in S$, then $\nu(S,T)=\nu(S\setminus\{i\},T)+b$; the left-hand derivative exponent is $a+b+\nu(S,T)$, equal modulo two to the right-hand side exponent $a+\nu(S\setminus\{i\},T)$. If $i\in T$, then $\nu(S,T)=\nu(S,T\setminus\{i\})+c$; the left-hand exponent $a+b+\nu(S,T)$ equals the right-hand exponent $|S|+b+\nu(S,T\setminus\{i\})$, since $i\notin S$ and $|S|=a+c$. For the right derivative, if $i\in S$ the identity $\nu(S,T)=\nu(S\setminus\{i\},T)+b$ makes the left exponent $c+d+\nu(S,T)$ equal to the right exponent $|T|+c+\nu(S\setminus\{i\},T)$; if $i\in T$, the identity $\nu(S,T)=\nu(S,T\setminus\{i\})+c$ makes both exponents $d+\nu(S,T\setminus\{i\})$ modulo two. If $S\cap T$ contains a common element other than $i$, all Leibniz terms vanish because an overlap remains. If $S\cap T=\{i\}$, both terms can be nonzero, but their exponents differ by one: for the left rule they are $a+\nu(S\setminus\{i\},T)$ and $|S|+b+\nu(S,T\setminus\{i\})$; for the right rule they are $|T|+c+\nu(S\setminus\{i\},T)$ and $d+\nu(S,T\setminus\{i\})$. Thus they cancel in both rules, matching the derivative of $e_Se_T=0$. For homogeneous coefficient-left tensors $a\otimes e_S$ and $b\otimes e_T$, the left derivative of their product has the common sign $(-1)^{|S||b|+|a|+|b|}$ multiplying the exterior left Leibniz expansion; the first tensor Leibniz term has sign $(-1)^{|a|+(|S|-1)|b|}$, which is equal, and the second has the additional $(-1)^{|S|}$ required by the exterior rule. For the right derivative, the first tensor Leibniz term has sign $(-1)^{|b|+|T|+(|S|-1)|b|}=(-1)^{|S||b|+|T|}$, while the second carries $(-1)^{|S||b|}$; these are exactly the two exterior right-rule terms. Thus the stated coefficient-left derivative extensions satisfy the graded-tensor left and right Leibniz identities. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 3.1 For homogeneous tensors $a\otimes e_S$, $b\otimes e_T$ and $c\otimes e_U$, the sign in $((a\otimes e_S)(b\otimes e_T))(c\otimes e_U)$ is $|S||b|+(|S|+|T|)|c|$, while the sign in $(a\otimes e_S)((b\otimes e_T)(c\otimes e_U))$ is $|T||c|+|S|(|b|+|c|)$. These exponents agree modulo two; associativity of $\mathcal A$ and step 1.1 then gives associativity of the graded tensor product. Combining its sign $|S||b|$ with supercommutativity of both factors gives the total sign $(-1)^{(|a|+|S|)(|b|+|T|)}$ when the tensors are exchanged, so the tensor product is supercommutative. [F1, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 3.2 The vectors $e_S$ are a specified basis, so each coefficient polynomial has a unique finite expansion. For a fixed $i$, grouping terms with and without $i$ and moving $\xi_i$ to the left by the signed product rule gives a unique expression $f=f_0+\xi_i f_1$ with $f_0,f_1$ independent of $\xi_i$; hence the linear coefficient extraction in [F3] is well-defined. Applying the four ordered coefficient maps successively to $e_S$ returns $1$ exactly when $S=\{1,2,3,4\}$ and $0$ otherwise. By linearity, the full measure extracts precisely the stated top coefficient. [F1, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1]
  | 
  | 4.1 Reversing a word in the four generators and exchanging each undotted generator with its dotted conjugate preserves the square-zero and anticommutation relations. The coefficient operation preserves parity and reverses products by [F4]. For homogeneous $a\in\mathcal A$ and $e_S$, moving $e_S^*$ back to the right of $a^*$ contributes $(-1)^{|a||S|}$, so the induced tensor formula is $(a\otimes e_S)^*=(-1)^{|a||S|}a^*\otimes e_S^*$, as stated in [F1]. This formula respects the graded-tensor relation: applying it to $(1\otimes e_S)(a\otimes1)=(-1)^{|a||S|}(a\otimes e_S)$ gives the same identity after reversing the factors. Applying the operation twice restores every coefficient and every generator, so it descends to an involutive anti-automorphism. [F1, F4, step 1.1, step 2.1] ∎
