  | **Given:** Smooth coefficient functions in $\mathscr S$, the ordered four-generator Grassmann algebra, and the operators and shifted coordinate in the chiral-superfield definition.
  | 
  | [F1] [[def-qftx-n1-chiral-superfield]]: $\mathscr S=C^\infty(\mathbb R^{1,3},\mathcal A\otimes\Lambda_4)$, the left derivatives remove the specified generator with the ordered sign, and $y=x+i\theta\sigma\bar\theta$ uses a finite Taylor substitution.
  | 
  | [F2] [[lem-qftx-grassmann-coefficient-calculus]]: the four left Grassmann derivatives are odd derivations and anticommute on the finite exterior algebra.
  | 
  | [F3] [[def-qftx-super-poincare-algebra-4d]]: $\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\alpha}$ are fixed numerical matrices and commute with all coefficient variables and spacetime derivatives.
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Proof technique:** direct expansion and coordinate change.
  | 
  | **Given:** [F1]–[F3], with all spacetime derivatives acting on smooth coefficient functions.
  | 
  | 1.1 The left derivatives with respect to distinct odd generators anticommute by [F2]; each $\partial_\mu$ commutes with them and with every other $\partial_\nu$ because the coefficient functions are smooth. Expanding the displayed formulas for $D$ and $\bar D$, the only nonzero mixed terms in $\{D_\alpha,\bar D_{\dot\beta}\}$ are $\{\partial^L_{\theta^\alpha},-i\theta^\gamma\sigma^\mu_{\gamma\dot\beta}\partial_\mu\}=-i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\partial_\mu$ and $\{i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\gamma}\bar\theta^{\dot\gamma}\partial_\mu,-\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\beta}}\}=-i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\partial_\mu$. Their sum is the stated mixed anticommutator. In each same-type anticommutator, the derivative-only terms vanish by [F2], the terms with one coordinate and one derivative cancel or are zero because the differentiated coordinate is of the other type, and the products of two identical-type odd coordinates vanish after symmetrization. Thus both same-type anticommutators are zero. [F1, F2, F3, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 With $y=x+i\theta\sigma\bar\theta$, the left derivative gives $\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}y^\mu=-i\theta^\beta\sigma^\mu_{\beta\dot\alpha}$: the minus sign comes from differentiating the rightmost odd factor past the left odd $\theta^\beta$. The finite Taylor definition and ordinary algebra therefore give, on functions expressed in $(y,\theta,\bar\theta)$, $\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}|_x=\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}|_y-i\theta^\beta\sigma^\mu_{\beta\dot\alpha}\partial_{y^\mu}$ and $\partial_{x^\mu}=\partial_{y^\mu}$. Substitution into $\bar D$ cancels the two $\theta\sigma\partial_y$ terms, leaving $\bar D_{\dot\alpha}=-\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}|_y$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.2 Direct expansion of $Q_\alpha$ against $\bar D_{\dot\beta}$ gives two opposite terms: $\{\partial^L_{\theta^\alpha},-i\theta^\gamma\sigma^\mu_{\gamma\dot\beta}\partial_\mu\}=-i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\partial_\mu$ and $\{-i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\gamma}\bar\theta^{\dot\gamma}\partial_\mu,-\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\beta}}\}=+i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\partial_\mu$, so $\{Q_\alpha,\bar D_{\dot\beta}\}=0$. All other terms vanish by the same independent-generator and odd-square identities. The two barred operators $\bar Q_{\dot\alpha}$ and $\bar D_{\dot\beta}$ also anticommute: their barred derivative parts anticommute, their $\theta\partial_x$ parts anticommute, and each barred derivative anticommutes with each $\theta$ factor because it differentiates a different generator. Therefore $\bar D_{\dot\beta}(\delta_{\epsilon,\bar\epsilon}\Phi)=0$ whenever $\bar D_{\dot\beta}\Phi=0$, accounting for the odd parameter crossing the odd derivative. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 3.1 Expand a function in the unique ordered basis of $\Lambda_4$ with coefficients in $C^\infty(\mathbb R^{1,3},\mathcal A)$. In $(y,\theta,\bar\theta)$ coordinates, $\bar D_{\dot\alpha}\Phi=0$ for both dotted indices means both left derivatives in the barred generators vanish. For a fixed generator, distinct basis monomials containing it map under its derivative to distinct basis monomials not containing it, with coefficients $+1$ or $-1$; hence its derivative has zero kernel exactly on the span of monomials not containing it. Applying this to both barred generators shows $\Phi$ is independent of $\bar\theta$. The remaining ordered basis is $1,\theta^1,\theta^2,\theta^1\theta^2$, so its coefficients are unique and give the four displayed component functions and parities. [F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 4.1 The transformed field is smooth because it is a finite linear combination of spacetime derivatives of smooth coefficient functions, and it is chiral by step 2.2. Applying the unique basis expansion in step 3.1 to this transformed field yields one and only one tuple of four smooth components. This is the component transformation defined in the chiral-superfield definition; no field equation or quantum operator-domain assertion is involved. [F1, step 3.1, step 2.2, algebra] ∎
REPAIR items/lem-qftx-supercharge-positive-energy-pairing.md: passes only after auto-repair — adopt the canonical form:
  | **Given:** $\mathcal H$, $\Gamma$, $Q$, the composition operator $H=Q^2$, and a finite-dimensional positive-energy eigenspace $E_\lambda$ as in the Statement. Assume Countable Choice for the imported adjoint and self-adjoint definitions.
  | 
  | [A1] Countable Choice is used by the imported unbounded adjoint and self-adjoint operator definitions. [[def-countable-choice]]
  | 
  | [F1] [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]: the complex inner product is linear in its first argument, conjugate-linear in its second and positive definite.
  | 
  | [F2] [[def-hilbert-space]]: $\mathcal H$ is complete in the norm induced by its complex inner product.
  | 
  | [F3] [[def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph]]: the domain of a composition $Q^2$ is $\{\psi\in D(Q):Q\psi\in D(Q)\}$.
  | 
  | [F4] [[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]]: self-adjointness gives $\langle Qx,y\rangle=\langle x,Qy\rangle$ whenever $x,y\in D(Q)$.
  | 
  | [F5] [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]: a densely defined operator is self-adjoint when its domain agrees with the domain of its actual adjoint and the values agree.
  | 
  | [F6] [[thm-of-square-roots]]: every positive real $\lambda$ has a unique positive square root $\sqrt\lambda$.
  | 
  | [F7] [[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]]: the nonzero complex scalar $\sqrt\lambda$ has a multiplicative inverse and scalar multiplication is associative.
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Proof technique:** operator-domain calculation.
  | 
  | **Given:** [A1], [F1]–[F7], and the domain, grading and anticommutation hypotheses in the Statement.
  | 
  | 1.1 If $\psi\in E_\lambda$, then $\psi\in D(Q)$, $Q\psi\in D(Q)$ and $Q^2\psi=\lambda\psi$ by [F3]. Thus $Q\psi\in D(Q)$ and $Q(Q\psi)=\lambda\psi\in D(Q)$, so a second application of [F3] gives $Q\psi\in D(H)$. Moreover $H(Q\psi)=Q(Q^2\psi)=Q(\lambda\psi)=\lambda Q\psi$. Therefore $Q$ maps $E_\lambda$ into itself. [F2, F3, given, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 The domain of $H$ is invariant under $\Gamma$: if $\psi\in D(H)$, then $\Gamma\psi\in D(Q)$ by hypothesis and $Q\Gamma\psi=-\Gamma Q\psi\in D(Q)$ because $Q\psi\in D(Q)$ and $D(Q)$ is $\Gamma$-invariant. Applying the anticommutation relation twice gives $H\Gamma\psi=\Gamma H\psi$. Hence $\Gamma E_\lambda=E_\lambda$, and $Q\Gamma=-\Gamma Q$ shows that $Q$ maps $E_\lambda^+$ to $E_\lambda^-$ and $E_\lambda^-$ to $E_\lambda^+$. [given, F3, step 1.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 3.1 For $\psi,\varphi\in E_\lambda$, both vectors lie in $D(Q)$ and $Q\psi\in D(Q)$, by [F2], the adjoint is taken in the complete Hilbert space; self-adjointness in [F5] and the identity in [F4] give $\langle Q\psi,Q\varphi\rangle=\langle Q^2\psi,\varphi\rangle=\lambda\langle\psi,\varphi\rangle$. By [F6], and since $\sqrt\lambda$ is nonzero in the complex field by [F7], division by $\sqrt\lambda$ therefore makes $Q$ an isometry on $E_\lambda$. Also $(Q/\sqrt\lambda)^2\psi=Q^2\psi/\lambda=\psi$ there. Together with the parity exchange in step 2.1, this proves that the restriction $U_\lambda:E_\lambda^+\to E_\lambda^-$ is an isometry with inverse the restriction from $E_\lambda^-$ to $E_\lambda^+$; it is consequently unitary and the finite dimensions agree. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 4.1 If $\psi\in\ker H$, then $\psi\in D(Q^2)$, so $Q\psi\in D(Q)$. By [F2], [F5] and the adjoint identity [F4], $\|Q\psi\|^2=\langle Q\psi,Q\psi\rangle=\langle Q^2\psi,\psi\rangle=0$. Positive definiteness in [F1] forces $Q\psi=0$. [A1, F1, F2, F4, F5, given, algebra] ∎
REPAIR items/thm-qftx-witten-index-beta-independence.md: passes only after auto-repair — adopt the canonical form:
  | **Given:** The graded pure-point decomposition, odd self-adjoint supercharge, compatible Hilbert basis, and trace-class heat operators for all $\beta>0$ in the index-data definition.
  | 
  | [F1] [[lem-qftx-supercharge-positive-energy-pairing]]: for each positive energy $\lambda$, $Q/\sqrt\lambda$ maps the even eigenspace unitarily onto the odd eigenspace, so their finite dimensions agree. 
  | 
  | [F2] [[def-qftx-graded-trace-class-index-data]]: the heat operator is diagonal with eigenvalue $1$ on the zero-energy basis and $e^{-\beta\lambda}$ on each parity subspace at energy $\lambda>0$; it is trace class, so its diagonal family in the supplied Hilbert basis is absolutely summable.
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Proof technique:** blockwise supersymmetric pairing.
  | 
  | **Given:** [F1]–[F2] and $\beta>0$.
  | 
  | 1.1 By [F1], $Q/\sqrt\lambda$ is a linear bijection between the finite-dimensional positive-energy parity eigenspaces, so $m_\lambda^+:=\dim\mathcal H_\lambda^+=\dim\mathcal H_\lambda^-=:m_\lambda^-$ for every $\lambda\in\Sigma$. On any supplied orthonormal basis of $\mathcal H_\lambda^\pm$, [F2] gives $\langle T_\beta e,e\rangle=e^{-\beta\lambda}$ for each basis vector. Therefore the positive-energy contribution to the signed basis sum at this fixed $\lambda$ is $e^{-\beta\lambda}(m_\lambda^+-m_\lambda^-)=0$. [F1, F2, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 By [F2], the full diagonal family is absolutely summable. For the partition into its energy blocks, the absolute value of each block sum is at most the sum of the absolute values in that block; hence the family of block sums is also absolutely summable. To see that its sum equals the original sum, fix $\varepsilon>0$ and choose a finite set of basis vectors whose omitted absolute tail is below $\varepsilon$. Only finitely many energy blocks meet that finite set, and the omitted absolute tail bounds the difference between the full sum and the sum of those complete block sums. Letting $\varepsilon$ decrease to zero proves that grouping by energy preserves the sum. Step 1.1 cancels every positive-energy block; each zero-energy even basis vector contributes $1$ and each zero-energy odd basis vector contributes $-1$. The remaining finite sum is $m_0^+-m_0^-$, independent of $\beta$. The proof uses no trace cyclicity and no positive-energy eigenvector outside the given discrete decomposition. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
